     3→
     4→/// Single source of truth for "is this host loopback?" across the mobile
     5→/// stack.
     6→///
     7→/// Pairing policy depends on this answer in two opposite directions, so it
     8→/// must come from one place:
     9→/// - Loopback is the *most* trusted channel for manual dev pairing (it never
    10→///   leaves the machine, so it may carry the Stack bearer token).
    11→/// - Loopback is *forbidden* in anything that arrives by QR or deep link: a
    12→///   scanned code pointing at `127.0.0.1` would make the phone dial itself,
    13→///   so the phone rejects it outright and the Mac never mints one.
    14→///
    15→/// Because this is a trust boundary, classification is byte-based, not
    16→/// string-pattern-based: hosts are parsed with the same libc semantics the
    17→/// dialer's resolver applies (`inet_aton` for IPv4 names, so legacy spellings
    18→/// like `127.1`, `0x7f.0.0.1`, and `2130706433` classify exactly as they
    19→/// dial; `inet_pton` for IPv6, so every compressed/uncompressed/mixed
    20→/// spelling of one address classifies identically). Anything that dials the
    21→/// local machine counts: `127.0.0.0/8`, the unspecified `0.0.0.0/8` (a TCP
    22→/// connect to it lands on loopback), IPv6 `::1` and `::`, and IPv4-mapped or
    23→/// IPv4-compatible IPv6 forms embedding those ranges. `localhost` and
    24→/// `*.localhost` names match with or without the trailing root dot.
    25→public struct CmxLoopbackHost: Sendable {
    26→    /// Creates the classifier. It is stateless: construct one inline wherever
    27→    /// a loopback decision is needed; every instance applies the same rules.
    28→    public init() {}
    29→
    30→    /// Whether `host` names the local machine.
    31→    /// - Parameter host: A bare host string (IPv4, IPv6 with or without
    32→    ///   brackets or a zone index, or a DNS name).
    33→    public func matches(_ host: String) -> Bool {
    34→        var normalized = host.trimmingCharacters(in: .whitespacesAndNewlines).lowercased()
    35→        if normalized.hasPrefix("["), normalized.hasSuffix("]"), normalized.count > 2 {
    36→            normalized = String(normalized.dropFirst().dropLast())
    37→        }
    38→        // A single trailing root dot is the fully-qualified spelling of the
